# Copy to .release-request (gitignored) and edit, then: open tools/release.command
#
# FORMAT. This file is `.`-sourced as bash by tools/release.command, after
# release.env has already set PATH — so it is code, not config, and anything you
# write here executes with the toolchain on PATH. One NAME="value" per line;
# `#` comments and blank lines are fine. LF endings only: a CRLF copy leaves a
# trailing \r inside the last value, and the cut aborts on a component name it
# cannot match. Only COMPONENTS, FLAGS and KEEP_WINDOW are read — a mistyped
# FLAG= is not an error, it is silently ignored, and the run proceeds as if you
# had set nothing.
#
# COMPONENTS — one or more of: cli gateway edge agent relay
#
# Cut in order. Each one's marker commit is pushed before the next starts —
# release.sh never pushes, and its release-origin guard refuses to cut while this
# repo is ahead of its remote, so an unpushed marker strands the rest.
#
# `all` is a real release.sh argument and is REFUSED here: it cuts every
# component inside one process with no push between them, so the batch ends
# sitting on several unpushed markers under a HEAD that reads [RELEASED: <last>]
# — the exact wedge the launcher exists to prevent. List them instead. Unknown
# names are refused the same way, before anything is built.
#
# FLAGS — required for a public cut. Accepted:
#
#     --public        Developer-ID signing + notarization AND the CVE gate
#     --apple         Developer-ID signing + notarization only
#     --vulncheck     the CVE gate only
#     --dry-run       build, sign and checksum into dist/ and stop: no tag, no
#                     GitHub Release, no upload, no marker commit — and the
#                     launcher skips its push path entirely
#     --bump-minor    move the minor instead of the default patch
#     --bump-major    move the major
#     --keep-version  pin versions/<comp> and mint a fresh stamp anyway; see the
#                     RUNBOOK section of that name. Refused in combination with
#                     --bump-minor, --bump-major or --force
#     --force         bump and cut even when the component source has not changed
#
# Leaving FLAGS empty is not "defaults", it is a trap. With neither --apple nor
# --vulncheck set, release.sh asks whether to cut a public release — and stdin IS
# a terminal in an opened .command, so an unattended run blocks on a prompt
# nobody is watching until someone closes the window. Answer it with anything but
# y, or let an empty answer through, and the cut publishes an AD-HOC signed,
# un-CVE-gated build to the PUBLIC channel, which is the worse of the two
# outcomes. Say what you want.
#
# A component whose source has not changed since its last cut resolves to the
# same stamp, whose tag already exists, and is refused. That is a no-op, not a
# failure to work around: drop it from COMPONENTS. Before reaching for --force,
# read the RUNBOOK's "Two releases can carry the same source SHA" — a re-cut from
# unchanged source creates a version pair that the stamp's SHA segment cannot
# tell apart, and triage pays for it later. If nothing in the component changed,
# what you actually want is a distribution-only republish, and that is NOT a
# launcher path:
#
#     bash tools/release.sh --distribute-only <comp> <stamp>
#
# run by hand. --distribute-only is recognised only as release.sh's FIRST
# argument, because it takes its component and stamp positionally — so it cannot
# be expressed in FLAGS at all.
#
# KEEP_WINDOW — leave the Terminal window open even when the cut succeeds.
#
# Unset (the default), the launcher closes its own window on a clean finish, so
# a batch you walked away from does not leave a dead window behind. It closes
# ONLY on RELEASE-EXIT:0 and ONLY the window whose tty is this process's own —
# a failed cut always stays on screen, and no other Terminal window is ever
# touched. .release.log is written and flushed before the close is even asked
# for, so nothing is lost either way; the log is the record, the window is not.
#
# Set KEEP_WINDOW=1 when you are debugging the cut itself and want the scrollback
# in front of you rather than in a file — release.sh's own output, the notarize
# chatter, and anything you were about to run in that window by hand. It is also
# the right setting when you are watching a run you may want to interrupt.
#
#     KEEP_WINDOW=1
#
# The launcher reads it from the environment too, but an opened .command does not
# inherit your shell's environment — LaunchServices does not carry it across — so
# this file is the setting that actually reaches a double-clicked or `open`ed run.
#
# Nothing to set to make the close happen: it is the default, and where it cannot
# work the window simply stays. That is a cosmetic outcome, never a failed
# release — a window that would not close never changes the status the launcher
# exits with, and never delays it by more than the two seconds it allows itself.
#
# IF A MACHINE EVER ASKS FOR PERMISSION. The close is an Apple Event, and Apple
# Events between applications are gated by Automation consent. This one should
# not be: osascript runs hosted by Terminal and its target is Terminal, and an
# app scripting itself is exempt — the close was measured here with no dialog and
# no Automation entry created. If some future macOS decides otherwise you will
# see, once, "Terminal wants to control Terminal". Allow it in
#
#     System Settings → Privacy & Security → Automation → Terminal → Terminal
#
# and it is not asked again. Deny it, or leave the dialog standing, and nothing
# breaks: the launcher gives the event two seconds, kills it, and exits with the
# code the cut earned. It will not sit on that dialog with a release already
# published — which is exactly why the deadline is there, and why the launcher
# never probes for the grant: probing for it is the thing that raises the prompt.

COMPONENTS="edge"
FLAGS="--public"
